-## Subtopics
-
-### Cell-tree
-A compact representation of ray dependencies between cells (flex-blocks), used for ray-task scheduling and traversal speedup. It is several orders of magnitude smaller than the full dependency list [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Moving Walls Algorithm
-The preprocessing step that partitions the scene, moving boundaries to balance load and accommodate data extents before the kd-tree is extracted [S1, unofficial].
+*   **NP-Completeness:** Determining the optimum order for caching cells in ray tracing is NP-Complete; therefore, optimizations are required for scheduling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Single Fetch Limitation:** It is not generally possible to create a schedule that fetches each cell only once for ray tracing algorithms [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Disproportionate Scenes:** The flexibility of cut axes is critical for scenes with disproportionate lengths on each side [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Construction involves preprocessing (region growing, cropping) and tree extraction. The NP-Complete nature of optimal caching means scheduling is heuristic [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The cell-tree (dependency graph) is significantly smaller than the full set of ray dependencies, reducing memory overhead [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Reduces end-to-end rendering time by balancing load across render nodes and minimizing network communication time [S1, unofficial]. Space skipping is automated via data extents, reducing runtime for later processing steps [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles massive volumes; load-balanced for distributed rendering; automates space skipping; works with both ray casting and ray tracing [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Construction is complex (requires moving walls, DP for load balancing); optimal caching order is not guaranteed [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Load Balancing:** The method is specifically designed to solve the Load Balanced Network Distribution (LBND) problem, minimizing end-to-end rendering time which is a function of the slowest render-node, image composition time, and network communication time [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Data Reduction:** By cropping empty space early in the pipeline, it reduces run-time for preprocessing, processing, and network data movement [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Efficiency:** The cell-tree (dependency graph) is several orders of magnitude smaller than the full number of dependencies it represents [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Determinism:** Provides a deterministic depth order for any given view direction, which is essential for correct alpha blending in distributed ray casting [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Hardware Support:** Supports both software ray tracing and hardware ray casting [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The flex-block tree was introduced in the context of massive data management for distributed volume visualization [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the Load Balanced Network Distribution (LBND) problem, where the goal is to minimize end-to-end rendering time by balancing the slowest render node, composition time, and network communication [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to support both software ray tracing and hardware ray casting in distributed clusters [S1, unofficial].
+The flex-block tree and partition method were introduced in the context of massive data management for distributed volume visualization [S1, unofficial]. The work addresses the limitations of static data distribution and traditional space subdivision methods which do not adequately handle load balancing in distributed rendering environments [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon concepts from kd-trees and dynamic programming for multipass partition problems [S1, unofficial].
 
